Why We Built ARIA

Product knowledge, coding best practices, lessons learned the hard way — all locked up in individual brains (people carry knowledge). New team members take months to learn it, and every team keeps solving the same problems again and again.

This is the problem ARIA solves. Instead of people carrying knowledge, agentic tools will carry the knowledge, backed inside an ARIA agent.

Let me walk you through the step-by-step actions taken by ARIA.

First, localization. If your app needs to support different languages and regions, ARIA can add that support for you the right way, the first time.

Second, REST APIs. ARIA can help you build APIs that follow RFC standards — no guessing, no shortcuts, just clean, standard work.

Third, error handling. ARIA helps every service return error messages in the same clear format, based on RFC standards, so no matter which team built the service, the errors look and feel the same.

Fourth, handling database tools.

All the best practices we already follow in every development cycle — the difference is, now you do not have to remember. Let ARIA carry them for you.

Now, agentic skills are only useful if people can find them. So we built one more thing: the ARIA Skills Marketplace.

Think of it like an app store, but for knowledge. It is one central place where every developer at PNC can go and see what is available.

In the marketplace, you can do five things.

One — you can discover skills. Browse what is out there and see what might help you today.

Two — you can learn from other teams. The knowledge one team shares becomes something every team can use.

Three — you can give feedback and ratings. If a skill helped you, say so. If it can be better, tell us. Every rating makes the skills stronger.

Four — you can add your own skills. And it is easy. You do not need to be an AI expert. If you know something valuable, you can share it with very little effort.

Five — and this is my favorite — you can reuse what already works. Why build something from scratch when another team has already solved it? Take it, use it, and spend your time on new problems instead.

